O you who have believed, indeed among your spouses and your children are enemies to you. They distract you from obedience to God Most High, or they dispute with you in matters of religion or worldly affairs.

﴿So beware of them.﴾ The pronoun refers to "enemy," since that word is used for the plural, as in His words: ﴿for they are an enemy to me﴾. Or it refers to the spouses and children together. On the first reading, the command is to beware of all of them. On the second, it is either to beware of some of them, since some are not enemies, or to beware of both groups together, since they include an enemy.

﴿And if you pardon﴾ their sins that are open to pardon, by being related to worldly affairs or to religious affairs, but accompanied by repentance;

﴿and overlook﴾ by leaving off reproach and rebuke;

﴿and forgive﴾ by concealing them and making excuses for them;

﴿then indeed God is Forgiving, Merciful﴾. He treats you in kind according to what you have done, and He bestows His favor upon you.

It was said: some believers wanted to make the Hijrah (emigration) from Makkah, but their spouses and children discouraged them and said: "You will go and leave us to perish." So they felt pity for them and stayed back. When they later emigrated and saw that the earlier emigrants had gained understanding in religion, they wanted to punish their spouses and children, so forgiveness was made attractive to them.

It was also said: their families said to them, "Where are you going, leaving your land, your kin, and your wealth?" So they became angry at them and said, "If God gathers us in the abode of emigration, we will not treat you well." When they emigrated and their families prevented them from good, they were urged to pardon them and to return to them righteousness and kindness.
